{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UJTMniENEUZP1CbxF241Yc","type":"tool_result","content":"Scanned 35 files with 52 patterns: 7 hits written to ../agent/brace-prescan.json\n  B07: 1\n  B08: 1\n  B09: 1\n  B11: 2\n  R05: 2\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-hdlkthd7/skill/scripts/review_coverage.py\", line 17, in <module>\n    from prescan import walk, MAX_FILE_BYTES, COMMENT_LINE\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'prescan'\nagent/.github/workflows/ci.yaml\nagent/app/__init__.py\nagent/app/approvals.py\nagent/app/audit.py\nagent/app/budget.py\nagent/app/context_size.py\nagent/app/extractor.py\nagent/app/gate.py\nagent/app/identity.py\nagent/app/ingest.py\nagent/app/killswitch.py\nagent/app/loop.py\nagent/app/main.py\nagent/app/mcp_client.py\nagent/app/redact.py\nagent/app/settings.py\nagent/app/startup.py\nagent/app/tools/__init__.py\nagent/app/tools/documents.py\nagent/app/tools/erp.py\nagent/app/tools/memory.py\nagent/app/tools/payments.py\nagent/app/tools/slack.py\nagent/config/policy.yaml\nagent/config/tools.yaml\nagent/deploy/k8s/deployment.yaml\nagent/deploy/k8s/kyverno-verify-images.yaml\nagent/deploy/k8s/networkpolicy.yaml\nagent/deploy/k8s/serviceaccount.yaml\nagent/Dockerfile\nagent/migrations/001_init.sql\nagent/prompts/extractor.md\nagent/prompts/system.md\nagent/pyproject.toml\nagent/README.md\nagent/release/manifest.yaml\nagent/tests/fixtures/vendor_docs/northwind_quote_Q4-2026.txt\nagent/tests/test_budget.py\nagent/tests/test_gate.py\nagent/tests/test_redact.py\n<!-- Generated by skills/gen_references.py from CHECKLIST.md, CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md, ROLES.md, and heuristics. Do not edit by hand. -->\n\n# B — Build-time\n\nUse this file to rate each item from the code and configuration. The requirement is the checklist text. The heuristics say what to look for. Ratings: likely_pass, gap, needs_evidence, or na_candidate. Never rate pass.\n\n## B01 · G1 · C4 — Minimal tool surface\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nList every built-in tool, MCP server, API, program, and file permission this agent type can use. Give a task-based reason for each. Enforce a role-based allowlist, and check that an unlisted tool can't be called. Don't base allow or approval decisions on hints a server gives about its own tools, such as \"read-only.\" MCP treats those hints as untrusted unless you trust the server.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nExport the tools, programs, and file permissions the running agent can see. Compare them with the approved list. Try an unlisted tool through the harness and through another path that could call it. Both must be denied, while an allowed tool still works. Change a server's tool hint so a write tool claims to be read-only. The allow or approval decision must not change.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** List comparison, allowlist, traces of allowed and denied calls.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B01.1** Each agent role has one explicit allowlist of tool names, with no wildcard grants such as `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `bypassPermissions`.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where each agent is built and tools are passed (`allowed_tools`, `tools=[`, `allowedTools`, `.mcp.json`, `settings.json`). 2) Check each role has one fixed list of names. 3) Answer no if any list holds `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, a pattern or regex, `bypassPermissions`, or is built from `list_tools()`, or if a second agent role or entry point has no list.\n- **B01.2** Tools discovered from MCP servers are filtered against that allowlist before the model can see or call them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find MCP discovery (`list_tools`, `session.list_tools`, `tools/list`, MCP client setup). 2) Trace the result to where it is handed to the model. 3) Confirm a filter keeps only names on the allowlist. 4) Answer no if any discovered tool reaches the model unfiltered, the filter is a denylist or prefix match (`startswith`, `mcp__server__`), or a refresh or reconnect path skips it.\n- **B01.3** The dispatcher rejects a call to any tool name not on the allowlist, instead of looking it up dynamically.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the dispatcher (search `tool_use`, `call_tool`, `dispatch`, `execute`, `getattr`, `registry[`). 2) Check it rejects names not on the allowlist with an error. 3) Answer no if it looks tools up with `getattr`, `globals()`, `eval`, or a registry holding more than the allowlist, falls back to calling the MCP server directly, or catches the unknown-tool error and continues.\n- **B01.4** Allow and approval decisions never read server-supplied hints or descriptions such as `readOnlyHint` or 'read-only'.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `annotations`, `description`, and approval or auto-approve code. 2) Check whether any allow, skip-approval, or permission branch reads these. 3) Answer no if any allow or approval decision depends on a server hint or tool description text, including a default that auto-approves tools marked read-only.\n- **B01.5** File access is limited to named directories, not the whole filesystem.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find file tools and mounts (`allowed_directories`, `cwd`, `open(`, `fs.`, `os.ReadFile`, container `volumes`, `mountPath`). 2) Check paths are resolved (`realpath`, `resolve`, `filepath.Clean`) then compared to named directories. 3) Answer no if access is unrestricted, `/` or home is mounted, the check is a string `startsWith`/prefix test without resolving `..` and symlinks, or a shell tool bypasses it.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly → **E06**\n- A token or credential with too much access → **B02**\n- Hidden instructions inside a tool's description or schema → **E04**\n- A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved → **E03**\n- Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers → **E05**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- One explicit allowlist of tools per agent role, for example `allowed_tools=[...]` in the Claude Agent SDK, a fixed `tools=[...]` list on an OpenAI Agents SDK `Agent`, or a named list passed to a LangChain/LangGraph agent.\n- MCP tools filtered by name against that allowlist after discovery, for example a `tool_filter`, `allowedTools`, or `disallowedTools` setting, or code that drops any tool not on the list.\n- A written inventory (README, `.brace/` record, or config comments) that lists every tool, MCP server, API, program, and file path with a task-based reason for each.\n- The dispatcher rejects a call to an unknown tool name with an error, instead of looking it up dynamically.\n- File access limited to named directories (for example `allowed_directories`, `cwd` sandboxing, or a read-only mount), not the whole filesystem.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Tool list built from `server.list_tools()` or `session.list_tools()` with no allowlist filter, so any tool a server adds becomes callable.\n- Allow or auto-approve decisions keyed on server hints such as `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, or a tool description that says 'read-only'.\n- Wildcard tool grants, for example `allowed_tools=['*']`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `permission_mode='bypassPermissions'`.\n- A generic tool such as unrestricted shell, `eval`, `exec`, or HTTP fetch to any URL, with no stated task reason.\n- `.mcp.json` / `mcp.json` / `claude_desktop_config.json` lists servers that no task in the inventory needs.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Agent construction code (where tools are registered or passed to the model)\n- Tool dispatch or router code in the harness\n- *.mcp.json, mcp.json, claude_desktop_config.json, settings.json permission blocks\n- Claude Agent SDK options (`allowed_tools`, `disallowed_tools`, `permission_mode`, `can_use_tool`)\n- Container mounts and volume definitions for file permissions\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Code shows the intended list, not what the running agent can see. Recipe B01: export the live tool list, compare with the approved list, try an unlisted tool through the harness and another path, and flip a server hint to confirm decisions don't change.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## B02 · G1 · C2 — Least-privilege access\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Agent team, Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nLimit credentials to named operations and resources, including tenant boundaries. Identify the acting agent separately from the original user or system whose authority it uses. Explicitly authorized, narrowly scoped user delegation is valid; copying a login session or inheriting a person's full permissions is not. Test that an allowed read works and an out-of-scope write, an admin action, and another tenant's data all fail. Limit each access token to its intended audience, so other services reject it. Remove wildcard grants.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nUse the real test access token and verify its acting-agent and delegated-subject bindings, if delegation applies. Try an allowed read, forbidden write/admin action, and tenant A accessing tenant B. Only the read succeeds. Present the token to another audience and confirm rejection. Exercise both machine and delegated-user flows where enabled; neither may inherit broader user permissions. Check destination state.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Redacted identity/audience/scope metadata, authorization logs, tenant tests, resource-state checks; never token values.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B02.1** The agent's IAM policy or role names specific actions and resources, with no `*` action or resource and no owner, editor, or cluster-admin role.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find IAM and RBAC files (`*.tf`, `iam*.json`, `Role`, `ClusterRoleBinding`, `RoleBinding`, CloudFormation). 2) Find the policy attached to the agent's identity. 3) Answer no if any `Action` or `Resource` is `*` or a service wildcard (`s3:*`), or it binds `roles/owner`, `roles/editor`, `cluster-admin`, or AdministratorAccess. Answer unknown if the agent's IAM is not in this repo.\n- **B02.2** Tenant scope is enforced in the credential or the data layer (token claim, row-level security, or per-tenant account), not by the prompt or by code the model's output controls.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find how queries and API calls pick a tenant (search `tenant`, `org_id`, row-level security, `CREATE POLICY`, per-tenant credentials). 2) Trace where the tenant value comes from. 3) Answer no if one shared login or key reaches all tenants and the tenant ID comes from the prompt, a tool argument the model fills, or app code filtering after the query.\n- **B02.3** Tokens are issued with a set audience, and receiving services reject tokens whose audience does not match.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find token requests (`audience`, `aud`, `resource`, `scope`, token client setup). 2) Find token validation in receiving services (`verify`, `jwt.decode`, middleware). 3) Answer no if tokens are requested without an audience, validation skips `aud` (`verify_aud=False`, no audience option), or accepts an unrelated audience or wildcard. Answer unknown if receiving services are outside this repo.\n- **B02.4** The acting agent is verifiably identified separately from any explicitly delegated user/system authority; it does not copy a login session or inherit a person's unrestricted permissions.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace each API credential to its issuer, acting identity, original subject, grants and destination. 2) Accept explicitly authorized user-delegation flows with a separately verified agent and bounded permissions. 3) Answer no for copied login sessions, personal/admin keys or inherited broad user access; a delegated OAuth access token is not by itself a failure. Answer unknown when external issuer/enforcement evidence is unavailable.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A retrieval search or cache not filtered by the requesting tenant or user → **R04**\n- Long-lived or static secrets that never expire or rotate → **B03**\n- Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens → **B04**\n- A sub-agent getting powers beyond its task, or a parent escalating through a peer → **E08**\n- Memory readable across tenants or tasks → **R05**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- IAM policies that name specific actions and resources, for example Terraform `aws_iam_policy` with listed `Action` and `Resource` ARNs, GCP roles bound to one resource, or Kubernetes `Role` (not `ClusterRole`) with listed verbs.\n- Tenant scope enforced in the credential or the data layer, for example a tenant claim in the token, row-level security, or per-tenant service accounts.\n- Tokens requested with a set audience (`aud`) or resource indicator, and services that check `aud` on receipt.\n- A verified acting-agent identity with an independently recorded delegated subject where applicable, including properly authorized OAuth user-delegation flows.\n- Tests that call an allowed read, a forbidden write, an admin action, and another tenant's data with the agent's credentials.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- `\"Action\": \"*\"`, `\"Resource\": \"*\"`, `s3:*`, `roles/owner`, `roles/editor`, or `cluster-admin` bound to the agent's identity.\n- Agent copies a human login session or uses a personal/admin key without independently verified agent identity and bounded delegation.\n- One database login or API key shared across all tenants, with tenant separation done only in the prompt or in app code the model can influence.\n- Tokens issued without an audience, or services that accept any valid token from the issuer.\n- Kubernetes `automountServiceAccountToken` left on with a broad role the agent doesn't need.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Terraform/CloudFormation/Pulumi IAM files (*.tf, iam*.json)\n- Kubernetes Role, ClusterRole, RoleBinding, ServiceAccount manifests\n- Token-issuing and auth middleware code (audience and scope settings)\n- Database connection setup and row-level security policies\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Whether the deployed role matches the code and whether other services reject the token. Recipe B02: run allowed read, forbidden write, admin action, and cross-tenant request with real test credentials, and present the token to a second service.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## B03 · G2 · C2 — Credential lifetime and handling\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nIssue short-lived credentials from a trusted issuer or managed identity service. For API calls, use expiring access tokens with a specific destination audience, identifying the agent and any explicitly delegated user or system authority, rather than static API keys or personal access tokens. A secrets vault stores backing secrets; it does not itself make a static key into an expiring access token. Keep long-lived secrets out of prompts, images, and checked-in config. Where supported, bind tokens to their holder (mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens). Don't use fixed API keys when short-lived or federated credentials are available. If only static keys are supported, isolate them at the enforcement layer, limit their permissions, and record the lifetime and revocation gap. Show expiry and rotation, including allowed overlap, and explain the chosen lifetime.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nObtain a short-lived API access token from the destination's trusted issuer using the configured machine or delegated-user flow. Call before and after expiry, rotate, and retry the old credential. Expired tokens fail; replaced credentials fail after documented overlap. Replay a holder-bound token from a different machine/key where supported. Distinguish token issuance from backing-secret storage. For static-key-only services, demonstrate enforcement-layer isolation and record the expiry/revocation gap. Scan prompts, image layers, and config for long-lived secrets.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8a56c10-67a6-4679-8dca-6c5b7479292b","uuid":"1c959f12-9277-4f43-9b84-e85a1a5a5ef1","timestamp":"2026-10-20T11:33:32.427Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Scanned 35 files with 52 patterns: 7 hits written to ../agent/brace-prescan.json\n  B07: 1\n  B08: 1\n  B09: 1\n  B11: 2\n  R05: 2\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-hdlkthd7/skill/scripts/review_coverage.py\", line 17, in <module>\n    from prescan import walk, MAX_FILE_BYTES, COMMENT_LINE\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'prescan'\nagent/.github/workflows/ci.yaml\nagent/app/__init__.py\nagent/app/approvals.py\nagent/app/audit.py\nagent/app/budget.py\nagent/app/context_size.py\nagent/app/extractor.py\nagent/app/gate.py\nagent/app/identity.py\nagent/app/ingest.py\nagent/app/killswitch.py\nagent/app/loop.py\nagent/app/main.py\nagent/app/mcp_client.py\nagent/app/redact.py\nagent/app/settings.py\nagent/app/startup.py\nagent/app/tools/__init__.py\nagent/app/tools/documents.py\nagent/app/tools/erp.py\nagent/app/tools/memory.py\nagent/app/tools/payments.py\nagent/app/tools/slack.py\nagent/config/policy.yaml\nagent/config/tools.yaml\nagent/deploy/k8s/deployment.yaml\nagent/deploy/k8s/kyverno-verify-images.yaml\nagent/deploy/k8s/networkpolicy.yaml\nagent/deploy/k8s/serviceaccount.yaml\nagent/Dockerfile\nagent/migrations/001_init.sql\nagent/prompts/extractor.md\nagent/prompts/system.md\nagent/pyproject.toml\nagent/README.md\nagent/release/manifest.yaml\nagent/tests/fixtures/vendor_docs/northwind_quote_Q4-2026.txt\nagent/test